{"evt":"note_created","id":"note-mqgwhrs2aom","workspace":"D:\\zonoid","title":"APPROVE: headless-drain deadlock fix (spawnSync to async spawn) clears single-attempt review","summary":"Reviewed impl /7 (commit fbe7ff4, base 3afae91) as the single-attempt code judge. APPROVE, HOLD for dispatcher merge to main (flat task — judge did NOT merge). All rubric items pass: (1) runDrain uses child_process.spawn returning a Promise, accumulates stdout/stderr capped 32MB, unref'd timer SIGKILLs on timeout, resolves once via settled-guarded finish() on both close and error, sync spawn throw caught and resolved as spawnError — no double-resolve, no unhandled rejection. (2) Non-blocking: spawn not spawnSync, nothing else blocks. (3) Governor: concurrentRunning++ before spawn, decremented in finally after await across all three drains (learner/judge/label) — no leak on throw; iterationsUsed monotonic by design; maxIterations/tokenBudget/maxConcurrency guards intact. (4) daemon.js L2209 fire-and-forgets runDueDrains(state).catch(()=>{}) in the unref'd 5-min setInterval, outer try guards sync throw; other sweeps untouched. (5) Integration test is the real bar: stands up a real http server, real spawned child HTTP-calls /judge/next back into it, and fires an INDEPENDENT /ping probe WHILE the drain is in flight (drain kicked off without await, probe at 150ms, drain awaited after) asserting ping.status===200 AND ping.ms<3000 — measures mid-drain liveness, not just final success. Sound that it fails on old spawnSync (sync block means child callback never served, drainPromise.then not a function). node --test test/headless-drain.test.js = 42 pass / 0 fail, regression test 387ms. (6) Scope: exactly 3 files, ORCH_HEADLESS_DRAINS isHeadlessEnabled() unchanged and default-OFF, zero installer/flag changes.","created_by":"main","valid_from":"2026-06-16T17:13:06.530Z","vec":[-0.11554904282093048,0.02626105211675167,-0.02579513192176819,0.029149482026696205,0.049205757677555084,-0.08934019505977